strong_answer:
Here are a few ways to convert this, depending on your needs. 

To use `async/await`, you must use the promise-based version of the file system module (`fs/promises` in modern Node.js).

### 1. The Modern Way (Returning a Promise)
Usually, when converting to async/await, we get rid of the callback `cb` entirely and just return the value.

```javascript
const fs = require('fs/promises');

async function combineFiles() {
  const a = await fs.readFile('a.txt', 'utf8');
  const b = await fs.readFile('b.txt', 'utf8');

  return a + b;
}

// Usage:
// combineFiles().then(result => console.log(result)).catch(err => console.error(err));
```
*(Note: You don't need a `try/catch` block here if you are returning a Promise, because any errors will automatically be caught and passed to the `.catch()` of the calling function).*

### 2. Keeping the Callback (If required by older code)
If your surrounding code still relies on the `cb` function and you are just refactoring the inside of the function, you can use a `try/catch` block like this:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs/promises');

async function combineFiles(cb) {
  try {
    const a = await fs.readFile('a.txt', 'utf8');
    const b = await fs.readFile('b.txt', 'utf8');

    cb(null, a + b);
  } catch (err) {
    cb(err);
  }
}
```

### 3. Bonus: The Optimized Way (Concurrent reading)
In your original code, `b.txt` waits for `a.txt` to finish reading before it starts. Because they don't depend on each other, you can read them at the exact same time using `Promise.all`. This is much faster.

```javascript
const fs = require('fs/promises');

async function combineFiles() {
  // Starts both reads at the same time
  const [a, b] = await Promise.all([
    fs.readFile('a.txt', 'utf8'),
    fs.readFile('b.txt', 'utf8')
  ]);

  return a + b;
}
```
